 "signs": [
  {
   "text": "THE BREAKWATER. Loch Ryan is a sea loch, sheltered enough that the ferries come in almost on tiptoe. The lighthouse on the end does not pretend it is needed on a clear night. It likes to be asked.",
   "x": 15.0,
   "y": 31.0
  },
  {
   "text": "FÀILTE GU ALBA / WELCOME TO SCOTLAND. Mind the lorries: this little village sees more trucks than a village of its size really ought to. The Gaelic means exactly what it says, which is a rare thing in signs.",
   "x": 23.0,
   "y": 19.0
  },
  {
   "text": "AISEAG GU BEUL FEIRSTE / FERRY TO BELFAST. About two and a quarter hours across the North Channel. The ship has a cafe, a lounge and a gift shop that sells nothing you need and everything you want.",
   "x": 15.0,
   "y": 19.0
  },
  {
   "text": "LOCH RYAN. A sea loch, and home to Scotland's last big wild native oyster bed. The oysters have been here since long before the ferries, and they have never once been late.",
   "x": 19.0,
   "y": 22.0
  },
  {
   "x": 23,
   "y": 33,
   "text": "BLUE PLAQUE: Loch Ryan was a Second World War military port. Surrendered German U-boats gathered here in 1945.",
   "kind": "plaque",
   "dx": -0.475,
   "plaque": "cairnryan_u_boats",
   "title": "Loch Ryan U-Boats"
  },
  {
   "x": 20,
   "y": 34,
   "plaque": "cairnryan_ptolemy",
   "title": "Ptolemy's Loch",
   "text": "Around AD 150 the Greek geographer Ptolemy drew a map of Britain, and on it he marked a bay called Rerigonius Sinus. Most scholars think that bay is Loch Ryan, which makes it one of the first places in Scotland ever written down. It has been quietly showing off about it ever since."
  },
  {
   "x": 18,
   "y": 31,
   "plaque": "cairnryan_ailsa_craig",
   "title": "Ailsa Craig",
   "text": "On a clear day, look north for Ailsa Craig, a round granite island off the Ayrshire coast. Its rare granite has been made into curling stones for well over a century, and tens of thousands of gannets nest on its cliffs. Sailors called it Paddy's Milestone, halfway between Belfast and Glasgow."
  },
  {
   "x": 25,
   "y": 28,
   "plaque": "cairnryan_dal_riata",
   "title": "Scots From Ireland",
   "text": "Ireland is only about 20 km from Scotland at the narrowest point of the North Channel. Around 1,500 years ago the Gaelic kingdom of Dál Riata spread over both shores, and the Latin name for the Irish, Scoti, ended up naming Scotland itself. So Gaelic came over by boat too, and without a ticket."
  },
  {
   "x": 32,
   "y": 21,
   "plaque": "cairnryan_southern_upland_way",
   "title": "Southern Upland Way",
   "text": "Down the coast at Portpatrick starts the Southern Upland Way, about 214 miles of hill, forest and moor to Cockburnspath on the North Sea. It was Britain's first official coast to coast long-distance path. Most people walk it west to east, so the wind can give them a helpful shove."
  }
 ],
